[2026-01-15T20:42:23.547354] Smith-Waterman has a classic wavefront dependency: H[i][j] needs H[i-1][j-1], H[i-1][j], H[i][j-1]. 

Options:
1. Row-parallel with sequential inner loop - limited parallelism, simple
2. Anti-diagonal wavefront - full parallelism, deterministic, more complex but bounded
3. Strip-based with overlap - complex synchronization, not worth it

Chosen: Anti-diagonal wavefront. Each anti-diagonal d has cells where i+j=d, all independent. Process diagonals sequentially, cells within diagonal in parallel. Deterministic because merge order is fixed by cell index.

Rejected alternatives:
- Row-parallel: only n-way parallelism, wastes cores on long sequences
- Task-graph: overkill for regular pattern, more LOC

Bounded patch: modify constructMatrix only, ~80 LOC change. findHighestScore can use parallel stream (simple addition). traceback stays sequential.

Worker count: ForkJoinPool.commonPool(), capped to Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors().
Small-N fallback: if n*m < 10000, stay sequential (overhead dominates).

